==========================================
DAY 16 NOTES — Text Processing: awk & Advanced grep
Date: 14-09-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- awk splits each line into fields ($1, $2, $3...), $0 = the whole line
- Default delimiter is whitespace; use -F: (or any character) for custom delimiters
- awk -F: works great on /etc/passwd since it's colon-separated
- Conditions filter by column value: awk -F: '$3 >= 1000 {print $1}' = real users only
- awk 'NR==5' prints line 5; NR>=5 && NR<=10 prints a range of lines
- grep -E enables OR matching (err|warn|crit) — plain grep treats | as a literal character
- grep -A/-B/-C show lines After/Before/Context around a match

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] awk '{print $1}' basic column extraction
[ ] awk -F: with /etc/passwd
[ ] awk with conditions ($3 >= 1000)
[ ] awk NR (line number filtering)
[ ] grep -E / -A / -B

MY OWN OUTPUT (fill this in after running commands yourself):
$ awk -F: '{print $1}' /etc/passwd | head -5
> 

$ awk -F: '$3 >= 1000 {print $1}' /etc/passwd
> 

$ grep -A 2 "Failed password" /var/log/auth.log
> 


